The {{nihongo|'''Imaichi Line'''|今市線|Imaichi-sen}} is a [[rapid transit]] line operated by [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]] in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. It is designated as '''Line 8''' of the Okami Metro network and is distinguished by its light brown color ({{color box|#d46f09|border=darkgray}}). The line runs from [[Okami-Tobu Airport (Terminal 1)]] in the northeast to [[Minami-Sasame]] in the south, spanning {{cvt|18.2|km|mi|abbr=on}} and serving 23 stations. The line functions as the primary [[airport rail link|airport access line]] for [[Okami-Tobu Airport]], connecting the airport terminals to the central business district and the broader Okami Metro network.

The Imaichi Line is one of two lines in the Okami Metro network that utilizes [[linear motor]] technology, the other being the [[Nagata Line]] (which has since been upgraded to conventional rolling stock). The linear motor technology was chosen for the Imaichi Line to allow for shallow tunnels with smaller bore diameters, which was essential for navigating the dense urban districts of [[Mitsu-Minato]], [[Sugamo]], [[Chiyoda]], [[Chuo]], [[Tama]], and [[Minami]] wards that the line traverses. The line operates 5-car '''Type D''' trains, the smallest rolling stock type in the Okami Metro system, with trains measuring approximately {{cvt|100|m|ft|abbr=on}} in length and {{cvt|2.6|m|ft|abbr=on}} in width.

The line serves 6 major interchange hubs, including connections to the {{OMSN|D|00}} [[Tedorigawa Line]] at [[Mizu-cho (I)|Mizu-cho]] and [[Kachidoki (D)|Kachidoki]], the {{OMSN|N|00}} [[Nagata Line]] at [[Amatsumara-machi (I)|Amatsumara-machi]] and [[Minami-Sasame]], the {{OMSN|S|00}} [[Shitayanagi Line]] at [[Kurama-machi (S)|Kurama-machi]] and [[Kachidoki (S)|Kachidoki]], the {{OMSN|G|00}} [[Tokushige Line]] at [[Kami-cho]], the {{OMSN|K|00}} [[Tokuyama Line]] at [[Hanazono (K)|Hanazono]], the {{OMSN|C|00}} [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]] at [[National Diet of Okaiken - West]], and the {{OMSN|A|00}} [[Asahi Line]] at [[Asahimachi Chuo (A)|Asahimachi Chuo]].

==History==

===Planning and rationale for linear motor technology===
The Imaichi Line was conceived in the late 1990s as part of the fourth expansion phase of the Okami Municipal Subway. The primary motivation for the line was to provide a rapid transit connection to [[Okami-Tobu Airport]], the city's eastern airport, which had been served only by buses and private railway connections. The Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau recognized that a dedicated airport access line would significantly improve the airport's accessibility and support the growth of air traffic in and out of Okami.

The route was designed to pass through some of the densest and oldest urban districts of Okami, including the historic [[Mitsu-Minato]] ward and the central [[Chiyoda]] ward. The geological and urban constraints of these areas presented significant challenges for conventional tunneling. The dense network of existing underground utilities, building foundations, and other subway tunnels meant that deep-bore tunneling would be prohibitively expensive and disruptive.

The solution was to adopt [[linear motor]] technology, which had been pioneered on the [[Nagata Line]] in 1990. Linear motor trains have a lower profile than conventional rotary-motor trains, allowing for smaller tunnel bore diameters. This enables tunnels to be constructed at shallower depths, reducing the need for deep-bore tunneling and minimizing disruption to surface structures. The [[Okami Metro 3000 series|3000 series]] linear motor trains were developed by [[Okami Heavy Industries]] specifically for the Imaichi Line, incorporating lessons learned from the 70 series used on the Nagata Line.

===Construction and opening===
Construction of the Imaichi Line began in 2001, utilizing shield tunneling methods with reduced bore diameters made possible by the linear motor technology. The shallow tunnel depths allowed for faster construction and reduced costs compared to conventional deep-bore tunneling, though the project still faced challenges in navigating the complex underground environment of central Okami.

The line opened on {{date|2006|df=y}}, operating between [[Okami-Tobu Airport (Terminal 1)]] and [[Minami-Sasame]] with all 23 stations completed simultaneously. The opening ceremony was held at the airport terminal station and was attended by municipal officials, airport authorities, and representatives of [[Okami Heavy Industries]]. The Imaichi Line was the eighth subway line to open in Okami and was designated as Line 8.

The 3000 series trains operated in 5-car formations from the beginning, reflecting the Type D classification and the smaller platform dimensions associated with linear motor infrastructure. The trains were equipped with specialized luggage racks to accommodate airport passengers, a feature unique to the Imaichi Line.

===Rolling stock replacement===
In 2025, [[Okami Heavy Industries]] delivered the first [[Okami Metro 7000 series|7000 series]] trains to the Imaichi Line, beginning the process of replacing the aging 3000 series linear motor trains. The 7000 series, also classified as '''Type D''' (5-car, {{cvt|100|m|ft|abbr=on}}, {{cvt|2.6|m|ft|abbr=on}} wide), represents the latest generation of small-profile rolling stock for the Okami Metro network. While the 7000 series retains the compact dimensions required for the line's tunnel infrastructure, it incorporates significant advances in energy efficiency, passenger comfort, and operational reliability.

The 3000 series trains are being progressively retired as the 7000 series is introduced. The transition is expected to be completed by 2027. The 7000 series features upgraded passenger information systems, improved accessibility features, and compatibility with the Satori-AI integrated passenger management system introduced following the 2018 privatization.

The line's average station spacing of {{cvt|804|m|ft|abbr=on}} is the tightest in the Okami Metro network, reflecting the line's role as a local urban connector and airport access line. The minimum inter-station distance of {{cvt|257|m|ft|abbr=on}} occurs between the two airport terminal stations, providing convenient inter-terminal transit for airport passengers.

