==Rolling stock==

The Imaichi Line operates two types of rolling stock, both classified as '''Type D''' (5-car, {{cvt|100|m|ft|abbr=on}}, {{cvt|2.6|m|ft|abbr=on}} wide):

* '''[[Okami Metro 7000 series|7000 series]]''': Introduced in 2025, the 7000 series is the newest rolling stock on the Imaichi Line, manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]]. The 7000 series represents the latest generation of small-profile trains designed for the linear motor infrastructure of the Imaichi Line. The trains feature upgraded propulsion systems, improved accessibility, and compatibility with the Satori-AI integrated passenger management system. The 7000 series is gradually replacing the legacy 3000 series.
* '''[[Okami Metro 3000 series|3000 series]]''': The original linear motor train used on the Imaichi Line since its opening in 2006. The 3000 series was developed by [[Okami Heavy Industries]] based on the earlier 70 series used on the [[Nagata Line]]. The 3000 series features linear motor propulsion, which allows for the smaller tunnel bore diameters used on the line. The trains are being progressively retired as the 7000 series is introduced, with the transition expected to be completed by 2027.

==Through-service==

At [[Kawabata Mon]] ({{OMSN|I|18}}) in Chuo-ku, the Imaichi Line connects via the {{cvt|4.18|km|mi|abbr=on}} '''Toukai–Imaichi Rail Link''' to [[Tamade Station|Tamade]] (TK01), the northern terminus of the [[Toukai Main Line]] and [[Tokuden Main Line]]. This standard-gauge link enables through-running airport express services (such as the ''Hirao Express'') connecting [[Okami-Tobu Airport]] through central Okami to the [[Toukai]] and [[Tokuden]] networks serving [[Tokuyama Hirao Airport]].


